<section class="hero">
  <h1>choudoufu <img class="hero-icon" src="choudoufu-inline-64.png" alt="" width="64" height="64"></h1>
  <p class="tagline">Ownership on the resource.</p>
  <p class="versions">Built on <strong>OpenTofu {{.UpstreamVersion}}</strong>
  (fork point
  <a href="https://github.com/opentofu/opentofu/commit/03743ce6e8"><code>03743ce6e8</code></a>).
  The <a href="https://github.com/INTENTIUS/choudoufu/releases">latest
  release</a> names both versions in its notes.</p>
</section>

<section class="lead">
  <p>choudoufu is OpenTofu with live resource markers. Each resource carries
  its own ownership record as ordinary cloud tags, and every plan rebuilds
  prior state by reading those markers back off the live system. Your
  configuration says what should exist and the cloud says what does.</p>

  <p><strong>Choudoufu state is allowed to be stale. OpenTofu state is
  not.</strong> That is the whole difference. A record here is rebuilt from
  the live system on demand, so a stale or missing one costs a re-read, never
  a wrong plan. Adoption is a tag you write and a rename is a tag you
  rewrite.</p>

  <p>You never configure a backend, take a lock, or perform state surgery.
  What genuinely cannot be read back from the cloud — an effect with no cloud
  twin, like a <code>null_resource</code> or a <code>time_*</code> — persists
  as one small record per resource in SSM Parameter Store, S3, or a local
  file. Those records are written with conditional writes rather than
  protected by a lock, so a race is a loud, named failure instead of a
  blocking mutex or a silent clobber.</p>

  <p>Everything else in this fork is stock OpenTofu, unmodified. The name is
  stinky tofu, which is off-putting on first encounter and grows on
  people.</p>
</section>

<section class="status">
  <h2>Where this stands</h2>
  <p><strong>Experimental, and AWS only.</strong> Of the thirty-six most
  commonly used AWS resource types, all but one are admitted. The gap that
  hurts is the connective tissue between them, such as
  <code>aws_ecs_service</code> and <code>aws_lambda_permission</code>.</p>
  <p>Type coverage is rarely what stops a configuration, though. A
  <code>for_each</code> over a data source, a <code>count.index</code> in a
  resource name, a <code>backend "s3"</code> block, or a CI pipeline that
  saves a plan file will each stop one first.
  <a href="compatibility.html">Will my config work?</a> is the honest list,
  and it is worth ten minutes before you try a migration.</p>
</section>

<section class="paths">
  <h2>Two paths</h2>
  <div class="path-cards">
    <div class="path-card">
      <h3><a href="migrate.html">Migrate an existing estate</a></h3>
      <p>You have an OpenTofu configuration managing live AWS resources and a
      state file you would like to stop having. Deleting the file is the easy
      half; binding the live resources is the half that needs attention.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="path-card">
      <h3><a href="start.html">Start a new estate</a></h3>
      <p>Nothing exists yet and choudoufu will create all of it. Install,
      declare a <code>live</code> block, apply, then confirm no state file
      was written.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="demo">
  <h2>See it prove itself</h2>
  <p>The demo is also the test suite. It stands up a real estate against a
  local AWS emulator, deletes the state file partway through, and shows the
  plans stay exact anyway. It needs Docker and takes about two minutes on a
  warm Go build cache. The exit code is the verdict.</p>
  <pre><code>bash live/e2e/run.sh --expect 5</code></pre>
  <p>Or hand this to a coding agent and let it run the whole thing.</p>
  <pre><code>Clone https://github.com/INTENTIUS/choudoufu, then do the following.

1. Confirm Docker is running (`docker info` must succeed).
2. If Go is installed, skip this step. Otherwise download the latest
   release tarball for this platform from
   https://github.com/INTENTIUS/choudoufu/releases, extract it, and
   export TOFU_BIN=&lt;absolute path to the extracted choudoufu binary&gt;.
3. From the repo root, run: bash live/e2e/run.sh --expect 5
4. Report each step's result as the script prints it, and the final exit code.

Exit code 0 means every claim the script makes about live resource markers
held. Non-zero means one of them did not. Report which step failed.</code></pre>
</section>

